%% Bars

(global variable (bar 0))

(bar headline 1)		Andante maestoso
(bar headline 13)		Rehearsal letter A
(bar headline 24)		Rehearsal letter B
(bar headline 25)		Poco stringendo
(bar headline 26)		Poco più mosso
(bar headline 34)		Incalzando
(bar headline 39)		Animando
(bar headline 41)		Rehearsal letter C
(bar headline 42)		Ritenuto
(bar headline 46)		Tempo I
(bar headline 50)		Rehearsal letter D
(bar headline 51)		Poco stringendo
(bar headline 55)		Tempo I
(bar headline 64)		Rehearsal letter E
(bar headline $)

(location headline)
	(current room #hall)
	(bold) (room header #hall) (roman)
	(if) (facing audience) (then)
		\(facing the audience\)
	(elseif) (facing sideways) (then)
		\(facing sideways\)
	(endif)

(redraw status bar)
	(status bar @status) {
		(if) (interpreter supports links) (then)
			(div @statuslinks) {
				(link [help]) help
				(link [look]) look
				(link [wait]) wait
				(link [score]) score
				(link [undo]) undo
			}
		(endif)
		(if)
			(current room #hall)
			(bar $Bar)
			~($Bar = 0)
		(then)
			(if) (did trainwreck) (then)
				($Progress = 25)
			(else)
				($Progress = $Bar)
			(endif)
			(progress bar $Progress of 74)
		(else)
			(status headline)
		(endif)
	}

(on every tick)
	(current room #hall)
	(bar $Bar)
	(if) ($Bar = 0) (then)
		(div @spacer) {}
		(select)

			You shake hands with the (#concertmaster). She sits
			down, and behind your back the rest of the (#orchestra)
			follows her example.

		(or)

			The lights dim, and the applause comes to an end. You
			hear a few coughs and excited whispers.

			(now) ~(audience is lit)
			(now) (#applause is nowhere)
		(or)
			Expectation is in the air.
		(or)
			(if) (facing audience) (or) (facing sideways) (then)
				(select)
					You should probably turn to face the
					(#orchestra) now.
				(or)
					A sharp cough from the (#concertmaster)
					at your side.
					(par)
					Better turn towards the (#orchestra)
					now.
				(or)
					(face orchestra)
				(stopping)
			(endif)
		(stopping)
		(par)
		(if) ~(facing audience) ~(facing sideways) (then)
			(select)
				The (#harp2) is waiting for your signal.
				(notice #harp2)
			(or)
				The (#harp2) is waiting for your signal.
				(notice #harp2)
			(or)
				The (#harp2) is patiently awaiting your signal.
				(notice #harp2)
			(or)
				The (#harp2) is awaiting your signal.
				A hushed whisper can be heard from the (#audience).
			(or)
				The (#harp2) is patiently waiting for your signal.
				The (#audience) is beginning to murmur.
				(par)
				The (#concertmaster) emits a discreet but pointed cough.
				(notice #concertmaster)
			(or)
				(if) (current gaze #concertmaster) (then)

					The (#concertmaster) returns your glance, visibly
					confused, and

				(else)
					The (#concertmaster)
				(endif)
				takes it upon herself to signal the (#harp2).
				(par)
				The (#harp2) plays (start the piece)
			(stopping)
		(endif)
	(elseif) (stuck in silence) (then)
		(div @spacer) {}
		(select)
			(link [everybody]) Everybody is sitting perfectly still in anticipation.
		(or)
			(link [everybody]) Everybody watches you in silent anticipation.
		(or)
			The silence begins to transition from artistic
			to awkward.
		(or)

			The claps are hesitant at first, like the first few
			drops of a deluge. Soon the entire hall is flooded with
			thunderous applause.

			(par)
			No, no, no, not here! The fools!
			(par)

			The (#orchestra) is dumbstruck. Some of the musicians
			have turned around to look at (link [sarah]) {Sarah}.
			The cheering (#audience) is blissfully unaware of what
			just happened.

			(now) (#applause is #in #hall)
			(now) (did trainwreck)
			(now) (audience is lit)
			(now) (bar 75)
		(stopping)
	(elseif) ($Bar < 75) (then)
		($Bar plus 1 into $Bp1)
		(enter bar $Bp1)
	(endif)
	(if) (bar 75) (then)
		(div @spacer) {}
		(select)
			Your right hand slowly returns to your side.
		(or)
			(if) ~(did trainwreck) (then)
				Suddenly,
				(if) ~(facing audience) ~(facing sideways) (then)
					applause, like a whip across your neck
				(else)
					a massive applause
				(endif)
				.
				(evaluate)
			(endif)
			“Bravo!” cries some particularly annoying member of the (#audience).
			(now) (#applause is #in #hall)
		(or)
			(select)
				A roaring (#applause) fills the concert hall.

				(par)

				The (#concertmaster)
				(if) (did trainwreck) (then)
					seems internally conflicted, but then she
				(endif)
				stands up, and the rest of
				the (#orchestra) follows her example.

			(or)
				(if) (facing audience) (then)
					(par)

					Eventually, the applause dwindles, and
					the audience settles down, eager for
					the next piece. You turn once more to
					the orchestra.

					(par)

					The rest of the concert proceeds in
					much the same way as the opening piece.

					(if) (did trainwreck) (then)

						Sarah collects herself soon
						enough, but there are other
						incidents.

					(endif)

					The audience remains exuberant, and

					(if) (final result 5) (then)
						you feel rather invigorated by
					(else)
						after a while you
						(if) (did trainwreck) (then)
							begin to feel grateful for
						(else)
							find yourself warming up to
						(endif)
					(endif)

					their puppy-like enthusiasm.

					(par)

					Encores, flowers. The doors open, and
					the concert hall is gradually vacated.
					Spectators and musicians congregate in
					small groups that drift into the night.
					As usual, you end up personally
					clearing the stage of chairs and stands
					before driving the long way home.

					(par)

					You sleep until early afternoon the
					next day.

					(spacer)

					(epilogue)
				(else)
					A roaring (#applause) fills the concert hall.
				(endif)
			(stopping)
			(if) ~(facing audience) (then)
				(par)
				(select)
				(or)

					Protocol demands that you turn around
					and face the cheering (#audience)
					(if) (facing sideways) (then) fully (endif)
					.

				(or)
					(now) ~(facing sideways)
					(face audience)
				(stopping)
			(endif)
		(stopping)
	(endif)

(spacer)
	(div @centerspacer) {
		\* \* \*
	}

(on every tick)
	(if) ~(percussionist standing) (then)
		(bar $N)
		($N > 47)
		($N < 60)
		(random from 0 to 3 into 0)

		(par)

		Steve, the (cymbalist), gets on his feet. He's watching you very
		attentively.

		(now) (percussionist standing)
	(elseif) (bar 60) (then)
		(par)
		The (cymbalist) discreetly sits down again
		(if) ~(did cue perc-54) (then)
			, with a worried look
		(endif)
		.
		(now) ~(percussionist standing)
	(else)
		~(did cue perc-54)
		(par)
		The (cymbalist) is still watching you.
		(par)
	(endif)

(on every tick)
	(now) ~(inhibit clbas-pre-17)

(handle the cymbals)
	(par)
	{
		(did cue perc-54)
	(or)
		(current gaze #percussionist)
		(now) (did cue perc-54)
	}
	(select)
		(if) (bar 54) (then)

			The (percussionist) acknowledges your cue
			(if) ~(percussionist standing) (then)
				, swiftly gets on his feet,
				(now) (percussionist standing)
			(endif)
			and plays the cymbals, forte fortissimo, on the third beat.
			(good cue)

		(else)

			Interpreting your glance as a go-ahead, the (percussionist)
			(if) ~(percussionist standing) (then)
				scrambles to his feet and
				(now) (percussionist standing)
			(endif)
			brings the two concert cymbals together in a resounding crash,
			(too early 54).
			(bad cue)

		(endif)
	(or)
		The (link [cymbals]) cymbals ring out again, on the downbeat
		(if) ~(bar 55) (then)
			, still (too early 55)
		(endif)
		.
	(or)
		The (percussionist) plays the cymbals on the third beat.
		(if) ~(bar 56) (then)
			He is consistently (too early 56).
		(endif)
	(or)
		The (link [cymbals]) cymbals ring out a fourth time, on the downbeat
		(if) ~(bar 57) (then)
			, again (too early 57)
		(endif)
		.
	(or)
	(stopping)
	(par)

(handle the cymbals)

(handle the timpani)
	(par)
	{ (did cue timp-71) (or) (did botch timp-71) }
	(select)
		%% This was already narrated when setting the flag.
	(or)
		(if) (bar 73) (then)

			The (timpanist) plays another loud note on the downbeat,
			still one bar too late. In the ensuing silence, he
			suddenly gets his bearings and switches to a (tremolo).

			(now) (force timpani tremolo)
		(else)
			The (timpanist)
			(if) (did botch timp-71) (then)
				, still disoriented, plays a
			(else)
				plays another
			(endif)
			loud note on the first beat of the bar.
		(endif)
	(or)
		The (timpanist) plunges into a solo (tremolo), fortissimo, on the tonic.
		(if) (did botch timp-71) (then)
			He is still (too early 73).
		(endif)
		(now) (force timpani tremolo)
	(or)
		With a
		(select) confused look (or) pained smile (stopping)
		, the (timpanist) extends his (tremolo) for another bar.
	(stopping)
	(par)

(handle the timpani)

(enter bar 75)
	(now) (bar 75)

(enter bar $N)
	(now) (bar $N)
	(div @secondary) {
		(div @barheadline) {
			(bar headline $N)
		}
		(if) (did accidentally cue [$DesiredBar $Instr]) (then)
			(narrate bad cue $DesiredBar $Instr)
			(bad cue)
		(endif)
		(now) ~(there's a problem)
		(narrate music $N)
	}
	(add audible objects to scope)
	(par)
	(if) ~(has opened the score) ($N = 9) (then)
		It occurs to you that you haven't opened the (#score) yet.
	(elseif) ($N > 1) ($N < 74) (#score is open) (then)
		(current page $Page)
		(score page $Page bars $B1 $B2 $B3)
		(if) ($N < $B1) (or) ($N > $B3) (then)
			You
			(if) ~(got bearings) (then) desperately (endif)
			scan the (#score {sheet music}) in front of you,
			but what
			(select)
				you're hearing
			(or)
				you can hear
			(purely at random)
			right now is nowhere to be found.
			(now) ~(got bearings)
		(else)
			(if) (got bearings) (then)
				A glance at the (#score) confirms that you're
				currently
				(select)
					listening to
				(or)
					hearing
				(then purely at random)
			(else)

				With a glance at the (#score {printed score})
				you get your bearings: This is

				(now) (got bearings)
			(endif)
			(if) ($N < $B2) (then)
				($N minus $B1 into $Offset)
				($Subpage = @left-hand)
			(else)
				($N minus $B2 into $Offset)
				($Subpage = @right-hand)
			(endif)
			($Offset plus 1 into $Nth)
			the (narrate nth $Nth) bar on the $Subpage page.
		(endif)
	(endif)

(narrate nth 1)		first
(narrate nth 2)		second
(narrate nth 3)		third
(narrate nth 4)		fourth
(narrate nth 5)		fifth
(narrate nth 6)		sixth

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
%% Musical structure

(at 0 there's #silence)

(start the piece)

	the arpeggiated chord that leads up to the first bar of the
	piece.

	(par)

	(face orchestra)

	As though it had a mind of its own, your right hand
	(if) (#baton has parent #stand) (then)
		picks up the (#baton) and
		(now) (#baton is #heldby #maestro)
	(endif)
	begins to trace out beats in the air.

	(now) ~(audience is lit)
	(now) (#applause is nowhere)
	(now) (got bearings)
	(enter bar 1)
	(stop)

(narrate music 1/2/3)

	The (#harp2) (select) is playing (or) continues to play (stopping) a
	series of soft, arpeggiated (link [chords]) chords , enhanced by
	(#pizzicatos) in the (violins), (#viola), and (#bassi).

	(par)

	The (#harp1) plucks the individual chord notes, up and down in
	soothing waves.

(entrance 4 #celli)

(narrate bad cue 4 $Target)

	Confused by your (bad glance $Target [#celli #harold]), one of the
	(#celli {cellists}) at the back emits a stray note, arco.

	(par)
	(now) (did botch celli-4)

(narrate music 4)

	(if) (did cue $Target) (then)

		(now) (did cue celli-4)

		Your timely (glance $Target [#celli #harold])

		acts like a reassuring prompt for (#harold) and the other
		(#celli {cellists}).

		(good cue)
		(par)

		They pour out their emotions in a sudden, gripping fortissimo.
		This is the (#mainmotif) of the piece, beginning with a
		descending major scale on the downbeat, molto espressivo.

	(else)

		Delicately at first, then with increasing confidence, the
		(#celli {cello section}) plays a descending major scale
		starting on the downbeat. This is the (#mainmotif) of the
		piece.

	(endif)

	(par)

	Meanwhile, the (#harps), (violins), (#viola), and (#bassi) continue
	their accompaniment, steadily, reliably.

(at 4 there's #mainmotif)

(narrate music 5)

	The final note of the (#descendingscale) lingers for a while, softer,
	but with undiminished emotional intensity.

	(par)

	The (#harps) and (strings) keep going, like clockwork.

(at 5 there's #mainmotif)

(narrate music 6)

	In the (#celli), the last part of the (#mainmotif) is repeated with an
	embellishment.

	(if) (did cue celli-4) (then)

		It is less intense this time, a calm reflection of the
		emotional turmoil that preceded it.

	(endif)

	(par)

	The soothing (#accompaniment) in the (#harps) and (strings) continues,
	unruffled.

(at 6 there's #mainmotif)

(narrate music 7)

	The (#celli) repeat the end of the (#mainmotif {motif}) again

	(if) (did cue celli-4) (then)
		. Now it is merely a faint echo
	(endif)

	, ending on a long, soft note.

	(par)

	The steady (#accompaniment) in the (#harps) and (strings) continues.

	(par)

	The soft note in the celli begins to crescendo, growing also in
	intensity, striving, reaching for more.

(at 7 there's #mainmotif)

(narrate music 8)

	(if) (did cue celli-4) (then)
		Another eruption of emotions in the (#celli), again fortissimo.
	(else)
		Another (#descendingscale) in the (#celli), now in fortissimo.
	(endif)

	This time, the (#mainmotif) appears in a lower, minor key, tinged with
	a lamenting quality.

	(par)

	The (#accompaniment) played by the (#harps) and (strings) continues in
	the same lulling spirit, but with subtle harmonic adjustments to fit
	the new scale.

(at 8 there's #mainmotif)

(narrate music 9)

	The final note of the scale lingers for a while in the (#celli {cello
	section}), persevering, but then, with an air of inevitability,
	decreases in intensity.

	(par)

	The (#harps) and (strings) persist in their steadfast (#accompaniment).

(at 9 there's #mainmotif)

(narrate music 10)

	The (#celli) restate the end of the preceding (#mainmotif {motif}),
	again with an embellishment, another introspective comment on the
	flare-up that came before it.

	(par)

	The (#accompaniment) in the (#harps) and (strings) continues,
	relentlessly, like time itself.

(at 10 there's #mainmotif)

(narrate music 11)

	Again, the (#celli) play a second echo of the end of the (#mainmotif
	{motif}), faint, sad, almost resigned. And again, that last, lingering
	note seems to gain new energy, eventually turning into a crescendo,
