#zuleika
(name *)		Zuleika
(proper *)
(female *)
(musician *)
(* is #partof #violin2)

(descr *)

			Zuleika, second violin, is in her fifties, short and
			skinny but somehow imposing. Her hair is black with
			occasional streaks of grey. She wears bifocals and owns
			a chain of ice cream shops.

#charlotte
(name *)		Charlotte
(proper *)
(female *)
(musician *)
(* is #partof #violin2)

(descr *)

			Charlotte, second violin, sometimes brings her
			mischievous but absolutely adorable twin girls to
			rehearsal. They have the same intense green eyes and
			fiery red hair as their mother, who says it's been
			running in the family ever since her great-grandmother
			immigrated from northwestern Italy.

#viola
(name *)		violas
(dict *)		viola section violist violists viole
(section *)
(* is #partof #strings)
(slice * 3)

(descr *)

			There are eight violists altogether.

#hypatia
(name *)		Hypatia
(proper *)
(female *)
(musician *)
(* is #partof #viola)

(descr *)

			Hypatia, viola, moved to Bournebrook Rill after
			retiring from her lifelong duty as librarian in some
			remote mill town. A plain, black evening dress
			accentuates her pallid complexion, and she watches you
			keenly with large, unblinking eyes.

#terrence
(name *)		Terrence
(dict *)		terence terrance terance terry
(proper *)
(male *)
(musician *)
(* is #partof #viola)

(descr *)

			Terrence, viola, is a guest musician from Toronto,
			where he actually works as a commodities trader. He
			went on some kind of sabbatical leave, to travel far
			and wide and see the world, and took his viola with
			him. Says he'll be in Bournebrook Rill for just a few
			more days, until he can offload that last pallet of
			mackerel.

#celli
(name *)		celli
(plural *)

(dict *)		cello violoncell cellist cellists section cellos
			chellos chello chelli

(section *)
(* is #partof #strings)
(slice * 4)

(descr *)

			All nine members of the cello section are present,
			including (link [harold]) Harold , the section leader.

#harold
(name *)		Harold
(proper *)
(male *)
(musician *)
(* is #partof #celli)

(descr *)

			Harold, leader of the cello section, is a jovial man in
			his early sixties. He's got a large, friendly-looking
			moustache and sideburns, and what appears to be a
			phalaenopsis pinned to his lapel.
			(select)

				Right behind him is his niece, (link
				[lydia]) Lydia .

			(or)

				He is also the owner of Harold's Garden
				Supplies, where (link [mika]) Mika and (link
				[peter]) Peter work.

			(then purely at random)

#lydia
(name *)		Lydia
(proper *)
(female *)
(musician *)
(* is #partof #celli)

(descr *)

			Lydia, cello, is in her early twenties, and studies to
			become a nurse. Her jet black hair keeps falling down
			over one of her eyes, in a manner that you would find
			immensely irritating if you were her. She is engaged to
			be married to (link [michael]) Michael .

#maartje
(name *)		Maartje
(dict *)		martje
(proper *)
(female *)
(musician *)
(* is #partof #celli)

(descr *)

			Young Maartje doesn't speak, so you don't know much
			about her, but the cello has given her a strong and
			beautiful voice. A couple of years ago, after
			rehearsal, you asked her if she'd like to perform a
			solo piece with the orchestra. She thought for a
			moment, and then responded with a few tentative bars of
			Kol Nidrei. That was the starting point of one of your
			most memorable projects with the orchestra, and her
			tranquil, sincere interpretation at the concert was
			nothing short of stunning.

#bassi
(name *)		bassi
(plural *)
(dict *)		double bass contrabass basses
(heads *)		bassi basses contrabass
(section *)
(* is #partof #strings)
(slice * 4)

(descr *)

			You've got two double basses in the orchestra: (#fred)
			and (#melody). Not as many as you'd wish, of course,
			but they're hard to find.

#fred
(name *)		Fred
(dict *)		freddy freddie strickland
(proper *)
(male *)
(musician *)
(* is #partof #bassi)

(descr *)

			Fred recently turned sixty, and has been a cornerstone
			of the orchestra since long before you entered the
			picture. It feels like he'll remain behind that double
			bass for another sixty years to come. But you've
			started to see signs of forgetfulness, which is
			worrying.

#melody
(name *)		Melody
(proper *)
(female *)
(musician *)
(* is #partof #bassi)

(descr *)

			Melody is (link [liu yang]) {Liu Yang} (no space) 's
			daughter, and you still think of her as a teenage girl,
			although she's well into her twenties by now, and in a
			steady relationship with (link [thomas]) Thomas .

			(par)

			She's also an experienced contrabass player... by the
			name of Melody. You pride yourself on never cracking
			jokes about people's names, but this has been a
			challenge.

#percussionist
(name *)		cymbalist

(dict *)		cymbal cymbals piatto piatti percussion steve steven
			percussionist

(heads *)		player
(male *)
(musician *)
(* is #partof #percussion)
(slice * 1)
(percussionist)		(link [percussionist]) percussionist
(cymbalist)		(link [cymbalist]) cymbalist

(descr *)

			Steve is a skinny young man with blond hair and
			freckles, who

			(select)

				works part-time as a waiter at Chez (link
				[anna]) Anna .

			(or)

				is a regular at (link [frank]) Frank (no space)
				's gym.

			(at random)

			A bit gawky, but conscientious, and a very
			versatile percussionist. Tonight he's been allotted the
			concert cymbals.

			(par)

			(bar $Bar)
			He is currently
			(if) (percussionist standing) (then)
				standing up
			(else)
				sitting down on a chair
			(endif)
			(if)
				~(did cue perc-54)
				($Bar > 26)
				~($Bar = 53)
			(then)
				, looking rather confused
			(endif)
			.

#timpani
(name *)		timpanist
(dict *)		timpani timpanist kettle drum drums rakesh
(timpani)		(link [timpani]) timpani
(male *)
(musician *)
(* is #partof #percussion)
(slice * 1)
(timpanist)		(link [timpanist]) timpanist

(descr *)

			Rakesh, timpani, is in his late thirties, and sports a
			shiny black pompadour and a trimmed beard. He makes his
			own mallets, and has maybe a dozen pairs of various
			sizes lined up on a table next to him.

			(par)

			(select)

				Every day, Rakesh takes his Labrador for a walk
				in the Botanic Garden, where they predictably
				run into (link [jeanette]) Jeanette and her
				Yorkshire Terrier.

			(or)

				He also likes to play badminton with (link
				[frank]) Frank .

			(then purely at random)

#harp1
(name *)		first harp
(dict *)		joy patterson arpa
(heads *)		harp
(female *)
(musician *)
(* is #partof #harps)

(descr *)

			Joy Patterson, first harp, is a portly woman in her
			fifties, with straight, black hair down to her
			shoulders. She used to be in a book club with (link
			[peter]) Peter and (link [liu yang]) {Liu Yang} , with
			regular meetings at Chez (link [anna]) Anna .

			(explain harps)

#harp2
(name *)		second harp
(dict *)		rebecca arpa
(heads *)		harp
(female *)
(musician *)
(* is #partof #harps)

(descr *)

			Rebecca Ward, second harp, is in her mid-twenties. She
			has her brown hair tied in a ponytail.

			(explain harps)

			(if) (bar 0) (then)
				(par)
				Rebecca acknowledges your cue, and plays
				(start the piece)
			(endif)

(dict (musician $))		musician player
(plural dict (section $))	sections

(singleton (musician $))
(singleton (section $))

(explain harps)
	(select)
		(par)

		The town council liked the idea of hosting a charity event for
		the Botanic Garden so much that, in addition to the venue, they
		insisted on throwing in a bit of cash. This you promptly spent
		on hiring two professional harpists for the evening: (#harp1
		{Joy Patterson}), who is teaching at the conservatory, and
		(#harp2 {Rebecca Ward}), one of her top students at the moment.

		(par)
	(or)
	(stopping)

(prevent [take/push/pull $Obj])
	($Obj has ancestor #orchestra)
	(no more seating $Obj)

(perform [talk to $])
	You open your mouth as if to speak, but then you think better of it.
	(tick) (stop)

(instead of [tell $Obj to | $])
	(try [talk to $Obj])

(understand [cry] as [shout])

(prevent [shout])

	You cry out loud. Then you immediately regret it, and try to cover it
	up with a cough.

	(if) ~(stuck in silence) (then)
		(par)
		People just look at you.
	(endif) %% when stuck in silence, we get "everybody watches you" next

(prevent [sing])
	(bar $Bar)
	You absent-mindedly hum
	(if) ($Bar > 0) ($Bar < 74) (then)
		along with the music,
	(else)
		a few notes, and
	(endif)
	then snap your mouth shut, hoping that nobody noticed.

