%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
%% The score

#score
(name *)		score
(dict *)

	extract sheet written music composition transcript facsimile print
	reprint broude brothers edition nutcracker casse noisette
	casse-noisette book printed

(score extract)		(* {score extract})
(openable *)
(singleton *)
(* is #on #stand)

(descr *)
		(if) (* is open) (then)
			(current page $P1)
			($P1 plus 1 into $P2)
			The score extract is currently open on pages $P1 - $P2.
			(par)
			(descr page)
		(else)

			A facsimile edition of the relevant pages from the
			Broude Brothers reprint of the original 1892 (italic)
			Nutcracker (roman) score. The composer's name is
			displayed prominently on the cover, lost in
			transliteration: Peter Ilich Tchaikovsky.

			(par)

			\[ This is the (link resource 1) {printed score} from
			your (italic) (story title) (roman) package. \]

			(par)

			You could (link [open score]) {open the score extract}.
		(endif)

(instead of [turn to *])
	(try [examine *])

(unlikely [turn *])

(understand [score] as [examine *])	%% ha!
	(* is in scope)

(perform [open *])
	(flip to 418)

(narrate closing *)
	You close the printed score.
	(now) (#currpage is nowhere)
	(now) (#nextpage is nowhere)
	(now) (#prevpage is nowhere)

(prevent [take *])
	The score is right where you want it, on the conductor's stand.

(descr page)
	(now) (current gaze #score)
	(current page $P)
	(descr page $P)
	(par)
	(no bar numbers)
	(par)
	You could look at the
	(if) ($P = 418) (then)
		(#nextpage)
	(elseif) ($P = 444) (then)
		(#prevpage)
	(else)
		(#nextpage {next}) or (#prevpage {previous}) page
	(endif)
	, turn to a given page number, or (link [close score]) close the score.

(global variable (current page 0))

(bar $Bar on page $Page)
	*(score page $Page bars $ $ $Last)
	~($Bar > $Last)

(score page 418 bars 1 3 8)
(descr page 418)

	(fixed pitch) No. 14. PAS DE DEUX (roman)

	(par)

	This piece calls for two (#flutes), a (#piccolo), two (#oboes),
	(#enghorn), two (#clarinets), (#bassclarinet), two (#bassoons), four
	(#horns), two (#trumpets), two (#trombones), (#basstrombone), (#tuba),
	(timpani), (link [cymbals]) cymbals , two (#harps), and a full
	(#strings).

	(par)

	The first eight bars are visible, two on the left-hand page and six on
	the right-hand page. They are mostly made up of a regular accompaniment
	in the (#harps) and (strings), initially set in motion by the (#harp2).

	(par)

	The main motif is introduced in the (#celli), fortissimo, in the second
	bar on the right-hand page.

	(if) ~(did cue celli-4) (then)
		(bar $Bar)
		(if) ($Bar > 3) (then)
			In hindsight, you should have cued this.
		(else)
			You will have to cue this, of course.
		(endif)
	(endif)

(score page 420 bars 9 14 16)
(descr page 420)

	In the five bars on the left-hand page you see a continued
	accompaniment in the (#harps) and (strings), and variations of the main
	motif in the (#celli). The last of these bars is marked with rehearsal
	letter A.

	(par)

	There are three bars on the right-hand page. The (#flutes) and
	(#clarinets) begin at the top, in fortissimo, and the (#oboes) and
	(#bassoons) enter two bars later.

	(bar $Bar)
	(if) ($Bar < 14) (then)
		Better cue both of these entrances, just to be on the safe side.
	(elseif) ($Bar < 16) (then)
		(if) ~(did cue flcl-14) (then)

			In hindsight, you should have cued the flutes and
			clarinets. Better cue the upcoming entrance in the
			oboes and bassoons, then.

		(else)

			You did cue the flutes and clarinets, and it's probably
			a good idea to cue the oboes and bassoons too, just to
			be on the safe side.

		(endif)
	(else)
		(if) ~(did cue flcl-14) (or) ~(did cue obbs-16) (then)

			In hindsight, you should have cued both of these
			entrances.

		(endif)
	(endif)

(score page 422 bars 17 19 20)
(descr page 422)

	There are two bars on the left-hand page. In the first, the (#horns)
	start to play chords in half notes. In the second, the main motif
	appears in the (#flutes), (#enghorn), (#clarinets), (#violin1), and
	(#celli).

	(par)

	In the first of the two bars on the right-hand page, the
	(#bassclarinet) enters with a sustained note.

(score page 424 bars 21 23 24)
(descr page 424)

	Four bars, two per page, are visible on this spread. The fourth is
	marked with rehearsal letter B. The music continues in the same general
	style as on the previous spread.

	(par)

	In the third bar, the (#bassoon1) starts to play a sustained note.

(score page 426 bars 25 27 32)
(descr page 426)

	Two bars are visible on the left-hand page. There's a stringendo in the
	first, leading to a tempo change, poco più mosso, in the second. The
	(#oboe1) plays a solo melody starting at the poco più mosso.

	(par)

	There are six bars on the right-hand page. In the fourth of them, the
	solo melody is taken over by the (#bassclarinet).

(score page 428 bars 33 36 38)
(descr page 428)

	There are six bars on this spread, three per page. The (#horns) are
	playing triplets the entire time. The second bar of the first page is
	marked “incalzando”.

	(par)

	At the start of the incalzando, the (#viola) and (#celli) introduce a
	yearning counter-motif, and the (#tuba) enters with a sustained,
	pianissimo note. The (horns) are joined by the (#flutes) and (#oboes)
	in the second bar of the right-hand page. One bar later, a (timpani)
	tremolo begins, pianissimo at first.

(score page 430 bars 39 42 45)
(descr page 430)

	There's an animando for three bars on the left-hand page, followed by a
	ritenuto for four bars on the right-hand page. The last of the animando
	bars is marked with rehearsal letter C.

	(par)

	At letter C, a grand fanfare begins in the (#trumpets) and
	(#trombones).

(score page 432 bars 46 48 49)
(descr page 432)

	Four bars, two per page:

	(par)

	In the first bar, marked Tempo I, the (#horns), (#trumpets),
	(#trombones), (#basstrombone), (#bassclarinet), (#tuba), and (#bassi)
	begin to play a series of loud half-note chords.

	(par)

	In the second bar, the rest of the (#orchestra) joins in with a
	climactic restatement of the main motif.

(score page 434 bars 50 52 53)
(descr page 434)

	Two bars are visible on each page of this spread. The first bar is
	marked with rehearsal letter D, and a stringendo begins in the second.

(score page 436 bars 54 56 57)
(descr page 436)

	Four bars, two per page. The stringendo ends with a Tempo I in the
	second bar on the left-hand page. Here, most of the orchestra is busy
	supporting the (#trumpets) as they repeat the main motif in the very
	Russian dynamic of forte forte fortissimo.

	(par)

	Each of these four bars contains a single, loud strike of the (link
	[cymbals]) cymbals .

(score page 438 bars 58 61 63)
(descr page 438)

	There are three bars on either page of this spread. The (strings) are
	elaborating on a wistful, sigh-like motif, while the (#harps) are doing
	arpeggios, and the (woodwinds) play chords.

	(par)

	The (#oboes) enter in the second bar, the (#flutes) and (#clarinets) in
	the third, and the (#bassclarinet) in the fourth.

(score page 440 bars 64 66 67)
(descr page 440)

	Four bars, the first of which bears rehearsal letter E. In the first
	two bars, on the left-hand page, the (strings) continue the wistful
	theme until it comes to a sudden stop at the beginning of the
	right-hand page. There, the (woodwinds) respond with a rapid clatter of
	sextuplets.

	(par)

	In the fourth bar, as everybody else goes quiet, the (#harps) enter
	with a loud arpeggiated figure.

(score page 442 bars 68 70 70)
(descr page 442)

	There are two bars on the left-hand page, and one bar on the right-hand
	page. The (#harps) continue with their arpeggios, while the (#brass)
	provides accompaniment in the form of long notes.

	(par)

	The (#violin1 {first}) and (#violin2 {second}) violins enter with a
	long, melodic note, fortissimo, in the first bar. In the second bar,
	the (#horns), (#flutes) and (#clarinets) join them, and in the third
	bar, the (#oboes) and (#bassoons) do the same.

(score page 444 bars 71 72 74)
(descr page 444)

	This last spread comprises four bars, one on the left-hand page and
	three on the right-hand page. (link [everybody]) Everybody is taking
	part in a prolonged, boisterous tonic, although the (#enghorn) and
	(#bassclarinet) don't join in until the first bar of the right-hand
	page. The piece ends with a bang on the third beat of the final bar.

	(par)

	The (timpanist) has brief, loud notes at the beginning of the first bar
	of each page, and a tremolo beginning in the second-to-last bar.

(no bar numbers)
