(dict *)	news newspaper morning local paper
(item *)
(openable *)
(* is #on #table)

(perform [examine/open/take *])
	You snatch (the *) off the table, and eagerly turn to the culture pages.

	(final result $Result)

	(div @clipping) {
		(review text $Result)
		(line)
		— (space) Ignatius E. Putterson, D.Mus.A.
	}

	(div @centerspacer) {
		(bold) \*\*\*
		(uppercase) (spell out $Result)
		(if) ($Result > 1) (then) batons (else) baton (endif)
		out of five
		\*\*\* (unstyle)
	}

	(if) ($Result > 1) (then)
		(par)

		A non-diegetic suggestion: There are many online videos of
		orchestras performing Tchaikovsky's Pas De Deux. If you haven't
		already done so, please go ahead and watch one now, to get a
		glimpse of yourself in action.

		(par)
	(endif)

	(game over)

(review text 1)

	If the Bournebrook Rill Community Orchestra has not exactly advanced,
	technically and musically, under the stewardship of Nevada Elmsbee, it
	has nevertheless not remained stagnant. Indeed, from the vantage point
	of last night's concert for the benefit of the Botanic Garden, the
	potential for amelioration has never been greater. If their story
	doesn't end here, one can safely look forward to the upswing for which
	the ensemble is inevitably headed.

(review text 2)

	In light of its role as a fundraiser for the new Mallard Habitat of the
	Botanic Garden, last night's sold-out performance by Nevada Elmsbee and
	the Bournebrook Rill Community Orchestra was, albeit not in the
	figurative, narrowly economical sense, for the birds. The subtle
	intricate complexities and emotional Sturm und Drang characteristic of
	late 19th century symphonic music were, on the whole, conspicuously
	absent. One must nevertheless acknowledge that for the most part, the
	ensemble did start and finish each piece in a roughly synchronous
	manner.

(review text 3)

	With maestro Nevada Elmsbee at the baton, the Bournebrook Rill
	Community Orchestra entered the stage, yesterday evening, in a
	philanthropic enterprise aimed at the Botanic Garden. Musically, the
	performance held together without any major incidents, and the general
	atmosphere was poignant, occasionally even venturing into lyrical
	territory. A certain lack of exactitude distracted from what could have
	been a riveting exposition of the symphonic ideas of the Romantic
	period. But the passion was there, and the grit and ambition. The
	result, on the whole, was not entirely unfavourable.

(review text 4)

	Last night the Bournebrook Rill Community Orchestra, with maestro
	Nevada Elmsbee at the helm, delved into a varied repertoire with
	passion and courage, and not without a certain degree of accuracy. A
	few rough corners must of course be expected, and excused, when amateur
	musicians are involved. Nevertheless, on the whole, the endeavour did
	come across as perhaps a little shaky, a little too ready to fall apart
	at the seams at the slightest mishap. One cannot help but wonder if the
	perceived level of musicality could not have been taken up a notch, if
	the orchestra had settled for a less challenging programme.

(review text 5)

	Organizers of charity events always run the risk of delivering such
	astounding performances that they, rather than the paying spectators,
	are remembered as the main benefactors. During last night's concert for
	the benefit of the Botanic Garden, maestro Nevada Elmsbee fell into
	precisely this trap. Technically immaculate and musically deep, the
	performance reduced the members of the audience to mere consumers,
	getting their money's worth of entertainment for the evening. They will
	be all but forgotten by future visitors to the Garden, who, as they
	come upon the new Mallard Habitat, will be admiring and rejoicing at
	the splashing, quacking, lasting legacy of the Bournebrook Rill
	Community Orchestra.

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
%% Gaze

(global variable (current gaze #maestro))

(global variable (did cue $))
(global variable (did accidentally cue $))

(global variable (will follow gaze $))

(try $)
	(now) ~(did cue $)
	(now) ~(did accidentally cue $)
	(fail)

(before [examine/cue $Obj])
	{ (facing audience) (or) (facing sideways) }
	($Obj has ancestor #stage)
	~{
		($Obj = #concertmaster)
		{ (bar 0) (or) (#applause has parent #hall) }
	}
	(first face orchestra)

(prevent [examine/cue $Obj])
	{ (there's a problem) (or) (stuck in silence) }
	{
		($Obj is one of [#violin1 #violin2])
	(or)
		($Obj has parent #violin1)
	(or)
		($Obj has parent #violin2)
	}
	Violins aren't the answer to this one.

(perform [examine $Obj])
	(cue $Obj @examine)

(perform [cue $Obj])
	(cue $Obj @cue)

(cue $Obj $Verb)
	(current gaze $OldObj)
	(now) (current gaze $Obj)
	(bar $Bar)
	(if) (will follow gaze $Follower) (then)
		(now) ~(will follow gaze $)
		(if)
			{ (musician $Obj) (or) (section $Obj) }
			~($Follower = $Obj)
			~($Follower is nested #partof $Obj)
			~($Bar = 75)
		(then)
			(link The $Follower)
			follows your gaze towards
			(the $Obj)
			(if)
				($Follower = #trumpet1)
				(michael may frown)
				($Obj is one of [#lydia #bassclarinet])
			(then)
				and frowns
			(endif)
			.
			(if) ($Obj = #tuba) (tuba is sleeping) (then)
				(It $Follower) notices that Anthony has fallen asleep,
				(if) ($Follower = #basstrombone) (then)

					and gives him a friendly but
					dream-shattering kick on the (italic)
					malleolus lateralis (unstyle). Then he
					leans forward to indicate a place in
					the sheet music on his dazed
					neighbour's stand.

					(now) ~(tuba is sleeping)
					(now) (inhibit confused tuba)

				(else)
					but is too far away to do anything about it.
				(endif)
			(endif)
			(par)
		(endif)
	(endif)
	(if) ($Bar > 0) ($Bar < 75) { (musician $Obj) (or) (section $Obj) } (then)
		(if) ($Obj = $OldObj) (then)
			You
			(select)
				continue to watch
			(or)
				keep watching
			(or)
				keep your eyes on
			(purely at random)
			(the $Obj).
			(if) ($Obj = #tuba) (tuba is sleeping) (then)
				(par)
				(select)
					Oh dear. The tuba seems to be
				(or)
					The tuba is
				(stopping)
				sound asleep.
			(endif)
		(else)
			(if) ($Verb = @examine) (or) ~(did describe $Obj) (then)
				(descr $Obj)
				(now) (did describe $Obj)
			(else)
				(you look at $Obj)
			(endif)
		(endif)
		(par)
		(cue $Obj at $Bar)
	(else)
		(if)
			($Verb = @cue)
			~{ ($Bar = 0) ($Obj is one of [#harp2 #harps]) }
			{ (musician $Obj) (or) (section $Obj) }
		(then)
			(you look at $Obj)
			(par)
		(endif)
		(descr $Obj)
		(now) (did describe $Obj)
	(endif)
	(if) (slice $Obj 3) ($Bar < 22) ~(tuba known to be sleepy) (then)
		(par)
		(if) ($Obj = #tuba) (then)
			You
		(else)
			Out of the corner of your eye, you
		(endif)
		notice the (#tuba) player stifling a yawn.
		(now) (tuba known to be sleepy)
	(endif)

(you look at $Obj)
	You
	(select)
		look at
	(or)
		turn your
		(select) eyes (or) gaze (purely at random)
		towards
	(purely at random)
	(the $Obj).

(silence or music)
	(bar $Bar)
	(if) (stuck in silence) (or) ($Bar > 73) (then)
		silence
	(else)
		music
	(endif)

(slice $Obj $Slice)
	($Obj is #partof $Parent)
	(slice $Parent $Slice)

(cue $Obj at $Bar)
	($Bar plus 1 into $Bp1)
	*(entrance $Bp1 $CueObj)
	(slice $CueObj $Slice)
	(slice $Obj $Slice)
	(now) (did cue $Obj)

(cue $Obj at $Bar)
	($Bar plus 2 into $Bp2)
	*(entrance $Bp2 $CueObj)
	~(immune at $Bar $CueObj)
	(slice $CueObj $Slice)
	(slice $Obj $Slice)
	~{ ($Bp2 = 26) ~($Obj is one of [#oboe1 #oboes]) }
	(now) (did accidentally cue [$Bp2 $Obj])

(cue $Obj at $Bar)
	($Bar plus 3 into $Bp3)
	*(entrance $Bp3 $CueObj)
	~(immune at $Bar $CueObj)
	(slice $CueObj $Slice)
	(slice $Obj $Slice)
	~{ ($Bp3 = 26) ~($Obj is one of [#oboe1 #oboes]) }
	(now) (did accidentally cue [$Bp3 $Obj])

(cue $Obj at $Bar)
	(entrance $Bar $Obj) %% Late cue, they won't be confused.

%% Particular places where people won't get confused.

(cue #bassclarinet at ($ < 17))
	(inhibit clbas-pre-17)
(cue #bassclarinet at 20)
(cue #bassoon1/#bassoon2 at 23)
(cue #celli at 34)
(cue #tuba at $Bar)
	{
		(inhibit confused tuba)
		(now) ~(inhibit confused tuba)
	}
	(or) (tuba is sleeping)
	(or) ($Bar > 21) ($Bar < 25)
	(or) ($Bar > 33)
(cue $ at 46)
(cue #percussionist at $N)
	($N > 47)
	($N < 60)
(cue #trumpet1/#trumpet2 at 54)
(cue #viola/#celli at 58)
(cue $ at 65/66)
(cue #enghorn/#bassclarinet at 71)
(cue #timpani at 72)

%% Otherwise, they do get confused.

(cue (musician $Obj) at $Bar)
	($Bar > 0)
	($Bar < 75)
	(now) (will follow gaze $Obj)
	(if) (current gaze $Obj) (then)
		(The $Obj)
	(else)
		(link The $Obj)
	(endif)
	(if) ($Obj = #bassclarinet) (then)
		(exchange looks $Obj #lydia)
	(elseif) ($Obj = #lydia) (then)
		(exchange looks $Obj #bassclarinet)
	(endif)
	(select)
		gives you a (confused adj) look.
	(or)
		returns your gaze with a (confused adj) look.
	(or)
		seems (confused verb) by your apparent cue.
	(or)
		looks back at you, (confused verb).
	(then at random)
	(notice $Obj)

(exchange looks $From $To)
	(select)
		(now) (avoid suggesting lydia)
	(or)
		was momentarily distracted by something in the
		(if) ($To = #lydia) (then)
			(link [cello section]) { cello section }
		(else)
			(link [woodwind section]) { woodwind section }
		(endif)
		. Now (it $From)
		(now) (michael may frown)
	(or)
		, after exchanging a quick glance with (link by name $To),
	(or)
		breaks eye contact with (link by name $To). (It $From)
		(now) ~(avoid suggesting lydia)
	(or)
	(stopping)

(link by name #bassclarinet)
	(link [kevin]) Kevin

(link by name $Obj)
	($Obj)

(confused adj)
	(select)
		quizzical
	(or)
		confused
	(or)
		confounded
	(or)
		perplexed
	(or)
