%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
%% Silliness

(prevent [$Verb (audible $Obj)])
	~($Verb = @examine)
	(The $Obj) (is $Obj) too abstract.

(no more seating $Obj)
	(The $Obj is) fine where (it $Obj) (is $Obj) (no space)
	—you've already spent hours on the (link [seating]) seating .

(perform [attack (animate $Obj)])

	There is much to be said for civility, of course, but one has to draw
	the line somewhere, and enough is quite simply enough. You charge at
	(the $Obj), drag (them $Obj) out of (its $Obj) chair, and give (them
	$Obj) a good, fair punch in the stomach.

	(outrage, not $Obj)

(prevent [kiss/feel (section $)])
	Your body language must be precise and deliberate. Please select one
	target at a time.

(perform [kiss/feel (animate $Obj)])

	These pent-up emotions, they simply cannot be subdued any longer! Your
	secret, confined for so long to the murky recesses of your heart,
	yearns for freedom. And you can certainly recognize a perfect
	opportunity when you see it. A sense of calm determination comes over
	you as you walk over to (the $Obj), and embrace (them $Obj)
	passionately.

	(par)

	To your chagrin, (the $Obj) pushes back, and expresses (its $Obj)
	hesitation with a series of increasingly disdainful invectives. In
	fairness, you may have overestimated the mutuality of the attraction,
	but it's nevertheless unfortunate that “get off me you pervert” has
	such a negative ring to it, especially when amplified by concert hall
	acoustics.

	(outrage, not $Obj)

(prevent [eat (current player $)])
	You consider nibbling on yourself, but reject the idea.

~(prevent [eat (animate $Obj)])

(perform [eat (animate $Obj)])

	It occurs to you that all human relations are ultimately governed by
	the law of the jungle: Eat or be eaten. And by acting now, you can be
	sure to have the element of surprise on your side. Cuspids bared, you
	charge at (the $Obj) and take a healthy bite from (its $Obj) forearm.

	(outrage, not $Obj)

(before [throw/attack #baton | $])
	(ensure #baton is held)

(perform [attack #baton])

	You snap the baton in half, and throw the pieces away. Who needs a
	baton anyway!

	(now) (#baton is nowhere)

(perform [throw #baton at #tuba/#basstrombone])

	The baton makes a beautiful arc through the air, and hits Peter, the
	bass trombone player, squarely in the eye. Luckily, it's the porcelain
	one, but Peter looks awfully upset anyway. He gets on his feet, and
	remains standing for a while, fuming with anger but not quite sure what
	to do. Then he storms out, slamming the door behind him.

	(now) (#baton is nowhere)
	(now) (#basstrombone is nowhere)
	(if) (current gaze #basstrombone) (then)
		(now) (current gaze #orchestra)
	(endif)
	(if) (will follow gaze #basstrombone) (then)
		(now) ~(will follow gaze $)
	(endif)

(perform [throw #baton at $Obj])
	(slice $Obj $Slice)

	(collect $M)
		*(musician $M)
		(slice $M $Slice)
	(into $List)
	(randomly select $Target from $List)

	The baton makes a beautiful arc through the air, narrowly misses (link
	the $Target), and falls to the floor somewhere behind (them $Target).
	Well, who needs a baton anyway.

	(now) (#baton is nowhere)

(perform [throw #baton at $])
	(try [throw #baton])

(perform [throw #baton])

	The baton makes a beautiful arc through the air and falls to the floor
	somewhere beyond your reach. Well, who needs a baton anyway.

	(now) (#baton is nowhere)

(outrage, not $Victim)
	(collect $M)
		*(musician $M)
		~($M = $Victim)
	(into $List)
	(randomly select $Defender from $List)
	(par)

	Some of the
	(if) ~($Victim = #putterson) (then)
		other
	(endif)

	musicians keep playing, with a sense of duty perhaps more accurately
	described as mental inertia, but most of them stop what they're doing
	and just gape.

	(par)

	(The $Defender) leaps to the rescue, armed with (its $Defender) stand,
	and starts beating you over the head in the very Russian dynamic of
	forte forte fortissimo. Others are quick to join in, to the unabashed
	thrill of the cheering audience, who are already splitting up into
	factions as the tumult escalates into a full-on riot.

	(par)

	The rest of the evening is a blur of sharp words, contemptuous looks,
	and strong hands dragging you feet first through damp, nocturnal
	streets. You wake up on the cold checkerboard tiles of a public
	restroom. Despite a screaming headache, you drag yourself up, and smile
	at your bruised reflection in the mirror. A couple of missing teeth are
	no big deal, not when you've finally had your breakthrough! This
	performance, like the premiere of Stravinsky's Rite of Spring, will go
	down in music history, and become the subject of countless meandering
	anecdotes, related to weary orchestras by sparkly-eyed conductors
	during the final fifteen minutes of every rehearsal from this day until
	the end of time.

	(div @centerspacer) {
		(bold) \*\*\*
		You are legend
		\*\*\* (unstyle)
	}

	(game over)

