%% Misc and meta

(instead of [read $Obj])
	(try [examine $Obj])

(understand command [xyzzy])
(understand [plugh/plover] as [xyzzy])
(perform [xyzzy])

	The anthropomorphic treble clef looks straight at you, service-minded
	to the point of pity.

	(par)

	— (space) It looks like you're trying to advance the story by means of
	magic thinking. Do you need (link [help]) HELP ?

	(par)

	Then it knocks on the screen from the inside, blinks a few times, and,
	in a sudden burst of artificial intelligence, vanishes.

(understand command [help])
(understand [verbs/hint] as [help])
(perform [help])

	To advance the story, type simple sentences at the prompt, such as
	(link [take baton]) {TAKE BATON}.

	(par)

	The default verb is EXAMINE; you may simply type (if) (interpreter
	supports links) (then) \(or click on\) (endif) the name of the thing
	you wish to inspect, such as the (link [audience]) AUDIENCE or the
	(link [first violins]) {FIRST VIOLINS}.

	(par)

	Other supported verbs include LOOK \(AT\), LISTEN \(TO\), WAIT, CUE,
	TAKE, OPEN, CLOSE, and TURN TO.

	(par)

	You can also (link [save]) SAVE , (link [restore]) RESTORE ,
	(if) (interpreter supports quit) (then)
		(link [quit]) QUIT ,
	(endif)
	or (link [restart]) RESTART the game
	(if) (interpreter supports undo) (then)
		, or (link [undo]) UNDO the last move
	(endif)
	.

	(par)

	Type (link [about]) ABOUT to see the credits for the game.

	(par)

	Three PDF files are distributed along with the game: A (link resource
	1) {printed score}, a pamphlet on (link resource 2) {how to make sense
	of the score}, and a diagram of the orchestra (link resource 3) seating
	. These “feelies” may be useful when tackling the puzzle, but they are
	not strictly necessary.

(understand command [about])
(understand [credits] as [about])
(perform [about])
	(banner)

	Written for the 25th Annual Interactive Fiction Competition
	\( (link resource @c) IFComp 2019 \).

	(par)

	(story release $RelN)

	This is Release $RelN of (story title), with post-competition bugfixes
	and improvements.

	(par)

	A thousand thanks to my beta testers:
	Mike Carletta,
	Brian Rushton,
	Marnix,
	Bikibird,
	Jason Guest,
	and Björn Paulsen.

	(par)

	The Nutcracker score is in the public domain, including reprints. The
	scanned version was generously provided by the Petrucci Music Library,
	(link resource @i) {IMSLP.org}.

(game over option)
	(final result $Result)
	($Result > 1)
	read a (link [note]) NOTE by the author,

(parse game over [note])
	(final result $Result)
	($Result > 1)
	(div @authornote) {

		Thank you for playing my game!

		(par)

		In a Pas De Deux \(steps for two\), a pair of dancers, usually
		a male and a female, perform ballet steps together. A similar
		kind of timing-critical interplay exists between conductor and
		orchestra, and between a work of interactive fiction and its
		reader.

		(par)

		The role of the conductor has of course been greatly simplified
		in the story, to improve gameplay. While it is true, in a
		sense, that it's a good idea to move and behave just as during
		rehearsals, a real conductor will also use their body language
		to communicate and tweak dynamics and timing in response to
		what the musicians are doing.

		(par)

		But knowing when and where to look is crucial. And you cannot
		observe without being observed.
		
		(par)

		(link resource @w) {Linus Åkesson}

	}

